(;GM[1]FF[4]CA[UTF-8]AP[otools-weiqi]SZ[19]KM[0]PB[Shi Xiang-Xia 施襄夏]PW[Fan Xi-Ping 范西屏]DT[1739]PC[Danghu (Pinghu), Zhejiang]GN[当湖十局 第七局]RE[W+R]DTX[自公开记录整理：Jim Yu (JZY) 1992 年发布于 rec.games.go，Smart Go 格式转 SGF]AB[dp][pd]AW[dd][pp]C[Game 7

White: Fan Xi-Ping

Black: Shi Xiang-Xia];W[qf];B[qk];W[qm];B[cf];W[ck];B[nq];W[fq];B[jq];W[fo];B[cm];W[of];B[mc];W[lq]C[Invasion to initiate a fight.  So far it's almost the same as Game 6, which was won by Shi \(played B too\).  It's understandable that Shi preferred the same openning, but Fan also decided to go with it -- he was of course prepared.  For example, an exchange of W P14 and B N17 \(more pressure on B R9\) before W M3.];B[no];W[kp];B[jp];W[kn];B[jo];W[ko];B[im];W[pr]C[Another improvement by Fan.  In Game 6, Fan played P3 and lost advantage in the early going.];B[eq]C[B does not play P2 but plays E3 instead, showing his aggressive attitude.  W Q2 hoped B to play P2 but failed, so later Fan improved this move once again: W would play Q2 right after W L4, then, without B K4, K5, and J7 stones, B is not strong enough to attack W left, so B would have to play P2 and W Q2 would get what he wants.];W[er];B[dr];W[fr]C[Although W E2 and F2 were common plays, but not appropriate under this situation.  Fan, playing W, later preferred E2 and E4 \(not F2\) to gain outside thickness, while Shi preferred W H3 \(not E2\), B F2, and W G2.  Both disagreed with W's plays in this game.  Now B has sente.];B[en]C[Attack, but somewhat "shallow."  B D9 would be good.  Then W C2 \(after B D9\) is good order, followed by B C3, a text move.  After that, \(W first\) D1, D3, F7, and C8.  Although B corner is hurt, he gains quite a bit at the left.];W[fn];B[em];W[fm];B[fl];W[gl];B[gm]C[Only move.];W[cr]C[Leave some aji at the corner; good timing.];B[ds];W[el]C[If atari at H7 instead, W would lose the outside potential.  Now E8, after B F9, W H7 would be a good order.];B[fk];W[hm];B[hl];W[gn];B[hk];W[in];B[jn];W[jm];B[il];W[hr];B[km];W[jl];B[jk];W[kl];B[lm];W[kk];B[mr]C[If M2 instead, after W N2, B L3, and W M4, B N1 would be a good move to create a ko.  Unfortunately for B, W has G9 and later F10 to create several ko threats, and B can't win the ko.  So he chose N2 instead of M2.];W[lr];B[ir];W[hq];B[ln];W[ks]C[L1 is the key point of liberties.];B[js];W[kq]C[Ko.];B[io];W[hn];B[ho];W[go];B[ek]C[B temporarily doesn't have enough ko threats, so it's necessary to defend here.  If not, W has E9, after B F10, W C6 is good move.];W[iq];B[kr];W[jr];B[ef];W[gc];B[qq];W[pq];B[qn]C[B R3 adn R6 are clever moves, but wrong time!  B R3 should play L2.  Since B now has plenty of ko threats, B would be able to thicken his outside.  *Then* R6 would be much more powerful.];W[pn];B[kr]C[Already late.];W[mq]C[Good move.];B[or];W[ms];B[nr];W[hp]C[To here, W not only "finishes" the ko, but also leave some weakness in B's shape.];B[pm];W[om]C[Atari at R5 instead would be a mediocre moe.  After \(B first\) S6, P7, Q8, and P6, when B N9, W would have to play L10, and B S5 would almost kill W corner.  Even if W would escape, there is no way for W to win.];B[pl];W[on]C[P7 and P6 are the only moves.];B[mk];W[kj];B[ol]C[P8 seems to be a text move, but it's almost the losing move.  B should play R5.  It \(R5\) doesn't just gain profit, but more importantly, it's directly related to the life/death of B's right.];W[rn];B[qo];C[W S6 and then R4 is a technique.]W[qp];C[No choice.  If S5, W would play S7 and B's liberties are one shorter.]B[rm];C[W gets the corner, plus L10 "sticking out," B is hurt badly. Also, B's right is not clean.  W has turned it around.]W[ro];C[Important.  Otherwise W C3 and B5 can live the corner, and B's territory would be definitely short.]B[cl];W[nm];B[ll];C[If W O7 or this Q10 played at R11, W would be enough.  Now Q10 is a fierce attack!  W is really showing his attitude.]W[pj];B[qj];W[pi];B[qi];C[W saves sentes at Q12, R12, and a cut at O8; R17 tests B's reaction, smart thinking and clever position.]W[qc];B[qd];W[pc];B[od];W[ph];B[nl];W[oc];C[B S15 and later S13 are to settle B's big group.]B[re];C[Calm.]W[rc];B[rg];W[rf];B[sf];W[rh];B[qh];C[Several W moves here perpare for coming O16.]W[qg];B[sg];W[nd];B[nc];W[md];C[B certainly wants to play M16, but W can cut at M17 since, if B M18, W has T12 and S16, then B R15 and W P15 is sente to prevent B's ladder.  After B S11, W L17.  B fails.  So M17 is a move when no other choice.]B[lc];C[W thus gets this good point.]W[jc];B[jd];W[id];C[Should play K15.  W would have to play N14, then B J15, W J17 and B M16; B goes out smoothly this way.]B[ld];W[mf];B[lf];W[rd];B[qe];W[je];B[kd];C[W K15 and J14 powerful.  Now B's left is thin.]W[if];B[he];W[ie];B[mg];W[oe];B[ri];W[ng];B[mh];W[nh];B[mi];W[me];C[W lives.  Although B also connects, W has started to lead.]B[kf];C[W invades with determination.  This is not just courage, but also percise calculations.]W[dg];B[df];C[W's way to live D13 is very smart.  First C3: it's not to live the corner, but to guarantee a sente at D9.]W[cq];B[dq];W[bo];B[cp];W[bp];B[br];W[bq];B[cs];W[di];B[eh];W[ei];B[fi];W[dk];C[Let's see why W D9 is sente: if B connects at E13 instead of D8, W D8 is sente to save get E8 back.  Then W has F10 \(B F9 three stones are to be cut at this moment\) and B can't kill W.]B[dl];W[bh];B[bl];W[dh];B[eg];C[W lives in B's territory; great skills!  Note: later if B C13, W B10 is key point to live.  Then, \(B first\) E10, D10, C11, C12, A9, B13, B14, and A13, W lives.]W[bk];C[B's left is destroyed, but B gets E17 to somewhat gain back his loss.]B[ec];W[ed];C[Beautiful order.]B[hc];W[hd];B[fc];W[fd];B[gb];W[gd];C[To here, either D17 or J17, B gets one.  So like W's success at teh left, B's invasion is also successful.]B[hb];C[Unnecessary.  There are not many ending game moves left, if W simply plays D17, D18 and then B16, he has won for sure.]W[fh];B[gh];W[dc];B[ic];W[fg];B[gf];W[gi];B[fj];W[gg];B[hg];W[hh];B[ff];W[gh];B[ig];W[ji];B[hf];C[Again, W should simply protect upper left corner.  Now K13 makes things complicated again.]W[jg];B[bg];W[ci];B[cg];W[bj];B[bd];W[bc];C[Good move.]B[bb];C[If A17, B can make seki after \(B first\) J12, J11, C18, B15, D18, K12, and K14.  Then W would lose the game.]W[cc];B[ih];W[ii];B[jh];W[kh];B[kg];W[jf];B[ac];W[be];B[af];W[cd];C[B D10 should throw in at L11, then W must capture L11.  The variation after that is extremely complicated.  If W is careful, he still wins, but a slight carelessness would cost W the game.  This is B's last chance and he missed it.]B[dj];W[ej];B[ah];W[ai];B[ch];W[ag];B[hj];W[hi];B[ah];W[eo];B[do];W[ag];B[ls];C[W finishes the ko.]W[ah];C[Too late.]B[ki];W[ip];B[lh];W[jj];C[Another ko, but the situation is already simple.]B[ae];W[ad];B[jr];W[hs];B[bd];W[dn];B[cn];W[ad];B[mp];W[is];B[bd];W[bs];B[as];W[ad];B[pg];W[pf];B[bd];W[fb];B[eb];W[ad];B[ps];W[qr];B[bd];W[db];B[fa];W[ad];B[oi];W[oh];B[bd];W[ea];B[da];W[ad];B[bf];W[ca];B[ea];C[W wins for sure.]W[ab];B[ql];W[nb];B[mb];W[ob];B[ce];W[bd];B[sn];W[rp];B[qs];W[rs];B[os];W[rr];B[bn];W[al];B[am];W[ak];B[ik];W[lo];B[mn];W[ni];B[nj];W[lj];B[oj];W[mj];B[na];W[sd];B[se];W[oa];B[ma];W[so];B[sm];W[ep];B[es];W[fs];B[co];W[ok];B[nk];W[pk];C[White wins.

Overview: At the beginning, B's attack at the lower left was successful, thus B began to lead.  B's mistakes \(#70 and #86\), however, cost him the lead.  W played very well afterwards: #93 and #95's sharp attack, and #99's great thinking put W on the winning track.  Later, during W's invasion to B's left and B's invasion to W's top, both players showed tremendous skills.  A spectacular game indeed.]B[ns])
